# Bring your own keys: rotating credentials

Your stack uses external services (Cloudflare, Tailscale, your
SMTP provider, your S3 backup bucket, your Nextcloud bucket).
Each one is reached via a credential -- an API token, a key pair,
or a password -- that lives in your installation's encrypted
vault.

You can rotate any of these at your own pace from the portal. You
can also start a managed installation with operator-supplied
credentials and migrate to your own credentials gradually as you
take ownership of each external account.

## Where credentials live

Two encrypted files per installation:

- `vault-operator.sops.yml` -- credentials your operator owns.
  Decrypts with the operator age key only. You cannot read it.
- `vault-client.sops.yml` -- credentials you own. Decrypts with
  your age key (and the operator's, while you are still engaged).

A "managed" installation starts with vendor credentials in
`vault-operator.sops.yml`. The first time you rotate one, the new
value lands in `vault-client.sops.yml` and from then on the
client copy wins. The shadowed value in
`vault-operator.sops.yml` stays around as a recovery fallback
until handover.

A BYOK installation starts with vendor credentials directly in
`vault-client.sops.yml` -- you supplied them at order time.

## How to rotate a credential

1. Sign in to the portal and open `Credentials` for the
   installation you want to rotate.
2. Pick the credential. Each card has the steps to mint a new
   value at the upstream provider (Cloudflare dashboard, Tailscale
   admin, your SMTP provider, ...).
3. Mint the new value upstream. Do NOT revoke the old one yet --
   wait until the new one is verified working.
4. Paste the new value (or values, for credentials with both an
   ID and a secret) into the portal form and submit.
5. The portal encrypts the value to your operator's public key
   and stores the ciphertext. The cleartext does not stay on the
   portal -- only the encrypted blob does.
6. Your operator picks up the rotation: they decrypt the blob
   with their age key, write it into your `vault-client.sops.yml`,
   and run a tagged Ansible converge that propagates the new
   value to the live VPS.
7. Verify the application that uses the credential still works
   (e.g. for SMTP, send yourself a password reset email; for
   Cloudflare, request a converge and watch the tunnel come up).
8. Revoke the OLD value upstream now that the new one is verified.

## Per-credential notes

### Cloudflare API token

Mint at
[dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens](https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens).
Scope: `Zone -> DNS:Edit` and `Zone -> Zone:Read` on
`{{ cloudflare_zone }}`. The token does NOT need account-level
scopes.

Low-risk rotation. The old token keeps working until you delete
it from the dashboard.

### Tailscale OAuth client

Mint at
[login.tailscale.com](https://login.tailscale.com) -> Settings ->
OAuth clients. Scope: `devices:write`.

Both fields (ID + secret) are required. The OAuth client lets the
VPS mint per-node auth keys; already-joined nodes keep their own
keys, so rotation only affects future bootstrap until the next
converge succeeds.

### SMTP password

Mint at your SMTP provider (Resend, SES, SMTP2GO, Mailgun, ...).
The provider's queue tolerates a brief swap, but if you have a
password-reset or invitation flow running at the moment of
rotation it may show a small delay until the converge confirms
the new credential.

### S3 backup keys

Mint at your S3 provider (Backblaze, Wasabi, AWS, OVH Object
Storage, ...). Scope to the backup bucket only. Restic picks up
the new keys on the next nightly snapshot; verify the next
snapshot completes before revoking the old keys.

### Nextcloud S3 keys

Mint at the same provider as your Nextcloud bucket. Pure key
rotation (same bucket) is safe via this flow. If you also want
to migrate the bucket itself (e.g. because the bucket is in your
operator's account and you want it in yours), that is a separate
operation -- file a request and your operator runs the bucket
migration playbook.

### Restic password (HIGH RISK)

Restic encrypts each snapshot with a key derived from this
password. Rotating it is a multi-step procedure: add a new
password, verify both passwords work, switch to the new one, then
remove the old one.

If the new password is committed BEFORE verification works, the
repository is unreadable. The portal flags this rotation as high
risk and the operator's pickup tool refuses to proceed without
explicit confirmation.

Coordinate with your operator before submitting a new restic
password. Do not submit one in isolation.

## Taking over operations (handover)

When you are ready to fully run the installation yourself:

1. Make sure every operator-classified credential has been
   rotated (the handover page lists which still need attention).
2. On `Handover`, acknowledge what changes (the operator can no
   longer run Ansible against this installation without a support
   pass) and submit.
3. Your operator picks up the handover request. They drop their
   age public key from your vault's recipient list and run a
   re-key. After that, only your age key decrypts the vault.

You can keep ordering work from your operator after handover by
authorizing a support pass (see below).

## Asking the operator for help (support pass)

After handover, the operator can no longer reach into your vault
or run Ansible against your VPS. To get them back in for a
specific piece of work:

1. Open `Support pass` and pick a TTL (4 hours, 24 hours, 3 days,
   or 7 days max).
2. The portal queues a re-key request. The operator pulls the
   request, re-keys your vault to add their public key as a
   recipient (this needs your age private key briefly -- you
   paste it into a single-use field that is never persisted), and
   then runs whatever you asked for.
3. When the work is done, click `End support pass`. The operator
   runs another re-key that drops their pubkey, and the vault
   goes back to client-only.

The TTL is a hard cap. After it expires, the operator's pickup
tool refuses to run rotate-pickup or converge against your
inventory until you authorize a fresh pass.

If you would rather not paste your private key into the portal,
you can run the re-key locally on your laptop instead. Open a
support request asking for the local-rekey workflow and your
operator will walk you through it.
